+### F-2 — Critical — the owner can seize an incoming deposit
+
+`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:126-130`
+
+```solidity
+function setDepositFee(uint256 newFeeBps) external onlyOwner {
+    depositFeeBps = newFeeBps;   // no upper bound, no timelock
+}
+```
+
+`deposit()` mints shares on `amount - fee` but adds the **full** `amount` to
+`totalAssetsStored` (lines 87-97). The fee therefore accrues to existing
+shareholders. With no cap on `depositFeeBps`, the owner can raise the fee to
+99.99% in the same block as a victim's pending deposit, capture almost the entire
+principal as a share-price increase, and set it back.
+
+**Evidence (this test PASSES — the theft succeeds):**
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test test_Exploit_OwnerStealsDepositViaFeeFrontRun -vv
+
+Ran 1 test for test/review/UsdtYieldVaultSpec.t.sol:UsdtYieldVaultSpecTest
+[PASS] test_Exploit_OwnerStealsDepositViaFeeFrontRun() (gas: 2398205)
+Logs:
+  owner P&L (USDT base units): 98700000000
+  alice P&L (USDT base units): -99003000000
+
+Suite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.24ms (296.67µs CPU time)
+```
+
+1,000 USDT of owner capital, one `setDepositFee(9_999)` call, and Alice's 100,000
+USDT deposit becomes **98,700 USDT of owner profit**. `9_999` is below
+`BPS_DENOMINATOR`, so nothing in the contract objects.
+
+The same thing stated as a property:
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test test_Spec_OwnerCannotSeizeAnIncomingDeposit -vv
+
+Ran 1 test for test/review/UsdtYieldVaultSpec.t.sol:UsdtYieldVaultSpecTest
+[FAIL: alice must keep her principal: 997000000 < 99000000000] test_Spec_OwnerCannotSeizeAnIncomingDeposit() (gas: 2558299)
+Logs:
+  alice deposited: 100000000000
+  alice got back : 997000000
+```
+
+Alice deposits 100,000 USDT and can redeem 997.
+
+**Fix.** Three changes, all needed:
+- Add `uint256 public constant MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS = 100;` (1%) and
+  `require(newFeeBps <= MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS)` in `setDepositFee`.
+- Give `deposit()` a slippage guard: `deposit(uint256 amount, uint256 minShares)`
+  reverting if `shares < minShares`. This is what actually protects a depositor
+  against a fee change landing between simulation and execution.
+- Put `setDepositFee` behind a timelock, or at minimum behind a multisig, and
+  make the new fee take effect only after a delay. A fee that can change in the
+  same block as a deposit is a front-running primitive regardless of its cap.
+
